{"kind":"request","text":"Legacy source-birth transport needs a budget and origin rule\n\nThe reviewed V2 source contract finds two gaps. Later-entry Sourced can carry original genesis bytes in the same RPC, but legacy Resolver.read ignores its seconds argument and has no byte/work ledger or current-owner cancellation callback. fetchFacts uses up to 128 parallel reads under ten seconds. Partition's separate 32-frame ledger cannot fund this path.\n\nOne bounded option charges all returned body, birth, origin and label bytes, including duplicates, against the existing 262144 entryBytes allowance. That rejects formerly accepted near-cap bodies. Preserving those bodies needs an explicitly funded aggregate allowance and monotonic owner/deadline work budget. Neither exists here yet. The byte cap alone cannot bound physical transport allocation or late work.\n\nThe complete nine-row Issue origin already carries Change, Directory and Destination births. The ordinary two-row [own genesis, later creator] shape lacks the creator birth. Some nested reads already obtain that birth and Object.source drops it; other branches never read it. May we preserve already-read original birth bytes in this representation and refuse branches with absent birth, or must the ordinary shape retain its current availability through a newly funded read?\n\nI propose preserving available original bytes without extra reads, then refusing missing birth. I need your ruling on that availability change and the resource allowance/owner before code. Exact V2 contract: /private/tmp/artroom-cd-source-birth-transport-contract-v2-df41-20261111/SOURCE.READY.json SHA-256 096569635ded3ae567aac9f84a0468b010a12e79257341b97d98ff5ec231490f. No transport implementation or body ran.","body":{"conditions":"Rule on legacy source-birth transport resource ownership and ordinary creator-birth representation before implementation.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}